# ==============================================================================
# Multi-block structured meshing (MESH_MODE 1) — an EIGHT-BLOCK TWO-RING O-GRID
# around a 2:1 ELLIPSE, whose middle ring was COMPUTED rather than drawn.
#
#   ./run.sh -conf config/multiblock_tworing_offset.dat
#
# This is where the #150 batch's two halves meet (issue #154). The seam edges
# declare `follows` (#151) on examples/geometries/ellipse_offset.dat, and that
# geometry is not a shape anybody drew: it is the shipped body offset by +0.25
# through app/services/geometry_offset.py (#152), the same one action the GUI's
# CAD ▸ Offset Geometry… performs. A circle's offset is a circle, which is why
# config/multiblock_tworing.dat next door could hand-draw its middle ring; an
# ellipse's offset is not an ellipse, and the nearest curve this tool can draw
# falls 3.39% short of the ring thickness at the nose.
#
# WHAT THE COMPUTED RING BUYS, in this file's own terms:
#   * the ring is a CONSTANT 0.25 thick all the way round, so the inner ring's
#     24 intervals mean the same thing at the nose as at the flank;
#   * its segmentation is the body's, by construction, so the four corners are
#     joints on both geometries and nothing had to be re-segmented;
#   * its faceting is the body's, so #94's divisibility advice has one geometry
#     to answer to instead of two.
#
# THE BUDGET IS THE SHIPPED O-GRIDS' — 4704 vertices, 9216 triangles, 192
# boundary edges. The comparison against a SINGLE ring on the same ellipse is in
# docs/design_notes/mesher.md and is the one #153 said its circle could not make.
# ==============================================================================

MESH_MODE 1
MESH_TOPOLOGY_FILE examples/topology/tworing_offset.json

# THREE geometries, and only two of them are boundaries. The body carries 'wall'
# in its sidecar and the far field 'farfield'; the computed middle ring carries
# 'seam', which reaches NOTHING — a following edge takes no condition, so if that
# label ever appeared in the .bnd it would arrive under its own name rather than
# hiding inside 'wall'.
GEOM_FILE examples/geometries/ellipse_body.dat
GEOM_FILE examples/geometries/ellipse_offset.dat
GEOM_FILE examples/geometries/ellipse_farfield.dat

# The wall spacing, under the name it already has. Only the INNER ring's radials
# take it; the outer ring's first cell is declared in the topology document,
# because the seam is not a wall.
BL_INITIAL_THICKNESS 0.001

MB_SPLIT_QUADS 1

# The FALLBACK. Both boundary geometries bind, so nothing should reach it.
BC_GEOM wall

EXPORT_VTK 1
EXPORT_STARCD 1
OUTPUT_FILENAME results/meshes/multiblock_tworing_offset/mesh_multiblock_tworing_offset.vtk
